{
 "id": "r2-joining",
 "title": "How letters join",
 "ar": "الحُرُوفُ المُتَّصِلَة",
 "goal": "Recognize letters in their joined shapes and spot where words break.",
 "noAutoVocab": true,
 "words": [],
 "understand": [
  {
   "p": "In handwriting and print, Arabic letters <strong>join</strong> to the letter after them, the way cursive does. So most letters have a start, a middle and an end shape. The core of the letter stays, and usually only the tail changes."
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "ب ← بـ ـبـ ـب",
     "en": "bāʾ: alone, start, middle, end"
    },
    {
     "ar": "ع ← عـ ـعـ ـع",
     "en": "ʿayn changes the most"
    },
    {
     "ar": "ه ← هـ ـهـ ـه",
     "en": "hāʾ also changes a lot"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "rule": "Six letters join only to the letter <em>before</em> them, never to the one after: <span class='ar'>ا د ذ ر ز و</span>. After one of these, the next letter starts fresh, which is why words seem to have gaps."
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "بَيْت",
     "en": "all joined: bayt"
    },
    {
     "ar": "دَار",
     "en": "د and ا don't join forward: dār"
    },
    {
     "ar": "وَرْد",
     "en": "no joins at all: ward"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tip": "Reading tip: find the <strong>dots first</strong>. In a joined word the bodies blur together, but the dots tell you which letter you're looking at."
  },
  {
   "h": "Tap to see the joined shapes"
  },
